=== B. Foils: which bodies does ELEM2 & GEOM2 exclude? ===
NOTE [written] B.CS_lemma  -- if Om is centrally symmetric about c in Om and x is an extreme point, x != c, then y = 2c - x is extreme and c = midpoint(x, y) is not a boundary state: for z in Om, c + 1*(c - z) = 2c - z is in Om by symmetry. So ELEM2 holds on every centrally symmetric body with two points (bit, square, hexagon, torus, Stiefel, disk, balls, the l2 ball).
NOTE [written] B.polytope_lemma  -- a polytope of dimension >= 2 is not relatively strictly convex (an edge midpoint is a boundary state), so it violates GEOM2 as soon as it satisfies ELEM2; a segment satisfies both.
PASS [witness] square.ELEM2  -- opposite vertices (1,1), (-1,-1): midpoint 0 strictly inside every facet, so not a boundary state
PASS [witness] square.GEOM2_fails  -- the pair face of (1,1), (-1,-1) is the square; (1,1), (1,-1) are distinct and their midpoint (1,0) is a boundary state (facet x <= 1 tight at it, strict at 0): not relatively strictly convex
PASS [witness] hexagon.ELEM2  -- opposite vertices: midpoint interior
PASS [witness] hexagon.GEOM2_fails  -- edge midpoint is a boundary state of the (whole-body) pair face
PASS [identity] pentagon.affine_regular  -- P_{k+1} + P_{k-1} = 2cos72 P_k: a linear image of the regular pentagon
PASS [enumerate] pentagon.ccw_convex  -- every vertex on the boundary, none outside
PASS [witness] pentagon.ELEM2  -- vertices 0 and 2 (non-adjacent): the midpoint of the diagonal is strictly inside every facet
PASS [witness] pentagon.GEOM2_fails  -- edge midpoint of [v0, v1] is a boundary state (facet tight there, strict at the centroid)
PASS [sample] bit.ELEM2  -- m = 0: 0 + eps(0 - z) = -eps z stays in [-1,1] for eps <= 1 (CS lemma); endpoints are extreme
NOTE [written] bit.GEOM2  -- the segment is strictly convex (kernel: relStrictConvex_of_strictConvex KF:617 with strictConvex_Icc): ELEM2 and GEOM2 hold.  Exclusion needs a further premise: HasTwoSharpTests (SharpTests.lean:41; hasTwoSharpTests_iff :155 gives 2 <= d) or ElementaryDrivability (not_drivable_Icc KF:529).
PASS [enumerate] trit.ELEM2_fails  -- every vertex pair is an edge; its midpoint has a zero coordinate and is a boundary state (witness the third vertex)
NOTE [written] trit.GEOM2  -- the pair face of two vertices is their edge, a segment: GEOM2 holds; the trit is not elementary
PASS [enumerate] bipyramid.vertices_in
PASS [witness] bipyramid.ELEM2  -- midpoint of the apexes is the interior point 0
PASS [witness] bipyramid.GEOM2_fails  -- midpoint of the edge [N, A] is a boundary state of the (whole-body) pair face of N, S
PASS [witness] bipyramid.capacity3  -- the three equator barycentric effects are >= 0 on every vertex, sum to one and perfectly distinguish A, B, C: ELEM2 alone does not bound capacity; GEOM2 does (note B.capacity)
NOTE [written] B.capacity  -- ELEM2 & GEOM2 => RelStrictConvex => capacity <= 2: if x1, x2, x3 were perfectly distinguishable by e1 + e2 + e3 = 1, then 1 - e1 is proper (0 at x1) and certain at x2 != x3, contradicting singleton faces (KF:590 from RelStrictConvex)
PASS [witness] torus.ELEM2  -- x = (1,0,1,0) on S^1 x S^1 (extreme) and -x: the centre 0 is not a boundary state by the CS lemma
PASS [witness] torus.GEOM2_fails  -- two extreme points with midpoint (1,0,0,0); with y = 0 the extension (1+eps)(1,0,0,0) has first-circle norm (1+eps)^2 > 1 for EVERY eps > 0 (exact sample shown; the inequality is linear in eps): flat face, not RSC
PASS [witness] stiefel.ELEM2  -- S1 (a 2-frame, extreme) and -S1: the centre 0 is not a boundary state by the CS lemma
PASS [witness] stiefel.GEOM2_fails  -- midpoint of the frames S1, S2 has first column e1 (norm 1); (1+eps) times it has spectral norm 1+eps > 1: flat face, not RSC
PASS [sample] cone.ELEM2_fails_sampled  -- apex-rim midpoints lie on the lateral surface, rim-rim midpoints in the base: boundary states (written: every pair of extreme points lies in a proper face)
NOTE [written] cone.GEOM2  -- pair faces are generating segments [apex, w] or the base disk: GEOM2 holds; the cone has capacity 3 (apex and two antipodal rim points) and is not elementary
NOTE [written] disk.ELEM2_GEOM2  -- the rebit disk is centrally symmetric and strictly convex: ELEM2 and GEOM2 hold.  Exclusion needs a further premise: corrected ElementaryDrivability (J must normalize SO(2) = the only flow; written, thread A / KINF-2 record) or the native-gate selector dim_of_nativeGate / dim_of_nativeGateOf (d in {1,3}).  The same holds for every ball of dimension >= 4 (spin factors): they need the DIM-1 selector.

=== C. Countercontrols (each must come out the other way) ===
PASS [witness] C1.ELEMprime_admits_qutrit  -- x = |0><0| and the mixed y = (|1><1|+|2><2|)/2 are perfectly distinguishable (P0, 1-P0) and their midpoint dominates 1/4 I (interior): a non-pure ELEM would put the qutrit in scope, where SF fails (census): "pure" is load-bearing
PASS [witness] C2.unlocalized_SF_code_path  -- ker(I - P) on C^3 has dimension 2: the same computation on the whole qutrit reports a non-singleton certain face
PASS [enumerate] C3.square_aut_count  -- exactly 8 of the 24 vertex permutations are affine: D4
PASS [enumerate] C3.square_auts_distinct  -- the 8 stored maps act as 8 distinct vertex permutations (guards against late binding of the coefficients)
PASS [enumerate] C3.square_extreme_transitive  -- ExtremeTransitive holds for the full automorphism group of the square
PASS [enumerate] C3.square_not_boundary_transitive  -- no automorphism carries the boundary state (1,0) to the vertex (1,1): BoundaryTransitive fails, so RelStrictConvex is load-bearing in Theorem A (kernel: not_boundaryTransitive_square2)
NOTE [written] C3.flow  -- the one-axis rotation flow of ball3 is RelStrictConvex but not ExtremeTransitive (the poles are fixed); kernel not_boundaryTransitive_flow (OrbitGeneration.lean:620): ExtremeTransitive is load-bearing
PASS [witness] C4.rectangle_vertices_pure  -- the four vertices (+-3/5, +-4/5, 0) are pure qubit states
PASS [witness] C4.rectangle_ELEM2  -- opposite vertices: centre interior
PASS [witness] C4.rectangle_GEOM2_fails  -- edge midpoint (3/5, 0) is a boundary state of the rectangle body: the geometric GEOM2 fails on this sub-body
PASS [witness] C4.SF2_quantum_effects_hold  -- the edge midpoint is a full-rank qubit state (>= 1/8 I); a qubit effect certain there has tr((I-E) rho) = 0 with rho > 0, so E = I, not proper: the effect form SF2 with quantum effects holds, and SEC with quantum effects fails at this boundary state
NOTE [written] C4.reading  -- the geometric principle (GEOM2, like SF with full effects, RelStrictConvex, K-inf-Trans) is not inherited by restricted-preparation sub-bodies of a quantum system; the effect form with quantum effects is.  Every geometric seam therefore presupposes that the completed body is the full state space of the system (state completeness), an unnamed reading of K-inf-Stage.
PASS [enumerate] C5.operational_capacity_is_2  -- with avail = {unit, P, 1-P} the qutrit has a perfectly distinguishable pair and no perfectly distinguishing triple (no three available effects sum to the unit)
PASS [witness] C5.SF_avail_fails  -- yet the available P is proper and certain on two states: "operational capacity 2 => SF(avail)" fails on a quantum model; the geometric ELEM2 excludes the qutrit body for every effect family (qutrit*.ELEM2_fails)

